  <section class="introduction">
    <p class="wide">
      The CLI exposes the Simple Java Mail builders as terminal commands. You can build a message, configure a mailer, validate it, test the connection,
      inspect SMTP capabilities, and send without writing a Java entry point.
      The send, connect, and probe commands finish only after their synchronous Mailer operation completes; send receipts are not printed.
      There is no CLI async switch in 10.0.0: remove <code>--mailer:async</code> and its option-specific help flag, both of which now produce an argument error.
      The daemon can still handle separate requests concurrently.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The CLI distribution and <code class="inline language-text">cli-module</code> require Java 17 or newer. The Java libraries require Java 11 or newer.
    </p>

    <h2>From builder API to CLI commands</h2>

    <p class="wide">
      Builder options are generated from the builder API and its Javadocs using <a href="https://picocli.info/">Picocli</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/dnault/therapi-runtime-javadoc">Therapi</a>, and a small adapter in Simple Java Mail.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Because the CLI is generated from the builder API, new builder options can become CLI flags without duplicating their documentation.
    </p>
  </section>

