  <a href="#section-how-to-use" id="section-how-to-use" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>How to use</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      To use Simple Java Mail CLI,
      <a href="download.html#navigation">Download</a>
      and expand the standalone-cli package and execute the <em>sjm</em> script.
      Included in the package are all dependencies and two helper scripts for Windows and Unix (generated by standard
      Maven plugins).
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Builder flags are deliberately explicit because they map directly to builder methods.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text small">
      sjm
      sjm --help
      sjm send
          --email:startingBlank
          --email:from "Mr. Candy" mrcandy@candystore.com
          --email:withSubject "About those tongue twisters"
          --email:to "Sweet Cheeks" "sweet.cheeks@candystore.com"
          --email:cc "Candy Research Group" "&lt;james@candy-insights.com&gt;;&lt;jean@candy-insights.com&gt;"
          --email:bcc "box1 &lt;info-box@candystore.com&gt;,box2 &lt;reminders@candystore.com&gt;"
          --email:withPlainText "I discovered a new flavor! (..)"
          --email:withTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery
          --mailer:withSMTPServer "my-smtp-server.com" 1234 "myuser" "mypass"
          --mailer:withTransportStrategy "SMTP_TLS"
    </code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      The REQUIRETLS flag is per Email and asks supporting relays to keep TLS mandatory after the first server accepts the message; it is separate from
      the Mailer's first-hop transport strategy. Exact EML uses <code class="inline language-text">--email:withExactTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery</code>.
      A send fails before <code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code> when the actual connection cannot honor the request. See
      <a href="security.html#section-requiretls">Require TLS for onward delivery</a> for the trust, capability, and reporting contract.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also check the <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/tree/develop/modules/cli-module/src/test/java/demo">demo code</a>
      included in the sources.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      <code class="inline language-text">sjm validate</code> applies the selected Mailer defaults and overrides and rehearses the complete message,
      including MIME construction, configured S/MIME, OpenPGP and DKIM processing, and the final encoded-size limit. It never connects to SMTP. Use
      Mailer options with the command when those settings are part of what you want to validate.
    </p>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-legacy-smtp-content" id="section-legacy-smtp-content" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Use a verified legacy SMTP route</h3>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">The CLI uses the same per-message negotiation as Java. If you have verified that your legacy server and its onward route preserve
      UTF-8 or 8-bit data without advertising support, enable this option:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">sjm send --email:startingBlank --email:from sender@example.org --email:to müller@example.org --email:withPlainText Hello --mailer:withSMTPServer legacy-relay.example.org 587 --mailer:withLegacySmtpContentSupport true</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">The equivalent loaded property is <code>simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport=true</code>; the default is false.
      The option participates in the daemon's Mailer configuration profile, so requests with different choices do not share a Mailer.
      Use <code>--mailer:withLegacySmtpContentSupport--help</code> for its full contract. TLS and content-safety checks remain enforced;
      successful submission is not proof of onward delivery. See the <a href="features.html#section-international-mail">international-mail guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-probe" id="section-probe" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Inspect an SMTP connection</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Use <code>sjm connect</code> for a simple connection check, or <code>sjm probe</code> to see what the server advertised and what happened
      during TLS setup. A probe opens a fresh connection, prints the same report as <code>mailer.sync().probeConnection()</code>, and closes
      that connection without sending an email. It connects even when logging-only mode is enabled.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-text">sjm probe --mailer:withSMTPServer smtp.example.org 587 --mailer:withTransportStrategy SMTP_TLS

# With SMTP settings and credentials already supplied through configuration:
sjm probe --authenticate

# Use the daemon's configuration and return the report to this terminal:
sjm probe --daemon=require --authenticate</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      SMTP authentication is off by default, even when credentials are configured. <code>--authenticate</code> opts in for this invocation
      only; it does not change later probes or sends. Configured proxy authentication and TLS client certificates still apply without that flag.
      Authentication follows your TLS settings, so check the endpoint and policy before opting in. Keep passwords out of shell history;
      use the existing <a href="#section-config-properties">configuration sources</a> instead.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The authentication flag controls the dedicated probe connection, not existing send pools. If you configured a nonzero connection-pool
      core size, building the Mailer can also open and authenticate pooled send connections. For a one-shot diagnostic without that warm-up,
      use <code>--mailer:withConnectionPoolCoreSize 0</code>, which is already the default, and check any per-cluster core-size overrides.
      This does not stop pools already running in a daemon.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-text">SMTP connection probe: SUCCESS
Endpoint: smtp.example.org:587 (smtp)
Greeting: 220 smtp.example.org ready
Before TLS: {SIZE=[10485760], STARTTLS=[]}
STARTTLS: completed
After TLS: {AUTH=[LOGIN PLAIN], DSN=[], SIZE=[26214400]}
...
Authentication: succeeded (LOGIN)
Elapsed: 142ms</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      This sample is abbreviated. The <a href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">SMTP diagnostics guide</a> explains the full report,
      including partial failures, unavailable metadata, and the separate capability lists before and after STARTTLS.
      Advertised support does not prove a later email will be accepted or delivered.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The report goes to stdout, including failed and unsupported reports. Exit code <code>0</code> means the probe succeeded;
      <code>3</code> means it failed or is unsupported. Invalid arguments return <code>2</code>. Ordinary execution errors are written to stderr;
      daemon routing retains its existing exit codes. A daemon may reuse the Mailer, but each probe still opens and closes its own SMTP connection.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Run <code>sjm probe --help</code> for Mailer options or <code>sjm probe --authenticate--help</code> for the authentication flag.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-daemon" id="section-daemon" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Optional background daemon</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Every <code class="inline language-text">sjm send</code>, <code class="inline language-text">connect</code>, <code class="inline language-text">probe</code>, and
      <code class="inline language-text">validate</code> command runs once and exits by default. Add <code class="inline language-text">-d</code>
      to acquire a per-user daemon. This keeps CLI setup and matching Mailers alive between commands.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">
      sjm daemon start
      sjm send -d ...
      sjm send --daemon=require ...
      sjm daemon status
      sjm daemon stop
    </code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      <code class="inline language-text">-d</code>, bare <code class="inline language-text">--daemon</code>, and
      <code class="inline language-text">--daemon=acquire</code> use a running daemon or start one. Use
      <code class="inline language-text">--daemon=require</code> when starting is not allowed, or
      <code class="inline language-text">--daemon=off</code> for an explicit one-shot command. Named instances are available through
      <code class="inline language-text">--daemon-instance=work</code> when separate startup configuration or lifecycle is needed.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      One daemon can safely retain several Mailers. The complete effective Mailer configuration selects the reusable entry, so different SMTP hosts,
      credentials, proxy settings, trust material, or pool settings do not share a Mailer. The standalone distribution includes
      <code class="inline language-text">batch-module</code>, which allows compatible daemon commands to reuse its SMTP connection pool. A custom
      classpath without that module remains supported, but cannot retain pooled SMTP transports.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The daemon is local to the operating-system user and authenticates every bounded request over a Unix-domain socket or a
      <code class="inline language-text">127.0.0.1</code> fallback. It is not a durable mail queue and never retries an outcome it cannot prove.
      The daemon is part of the CLI, so <code class="inline language-text">-d</code> is the normal cross-platform background route. The portable
      archive includes an optional tested systemd user unit. Homebrew and Chocolatey distribution is tracked separately in
      <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/issues/708">issue #708</a>; Windows does not require a Scheduled Task or Service. See the
      <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/develop/modules/cli-module/DAEMON.md">daemon operations guide</a> for configuration,
      pooling, shutdown, exit codes, and package-manager status.
    </p>
  </section>


